RDG_SYSTEM :: Polya–Carlson_Dichotomy (Relational)

Given RDG system:
    S = 1
    I = (a_n) ⊂ ℤ
    D = analytic / meromorphic continuation of f

Exactly one holds:

    (A) ARC-EXTENDABLE:
        ∃ open γ ⊂ B with continuation
            ⇒ f rational
            ⇒ poles at roots of unity
            ⇒ I satisfies linear recurrence.

    (B) NATURAL_BOUNDARY:
        No such γ exists
            ⇒ B is a natural boundary.

------------------------------------------------------------
TYPES
------------------------------------------------------------
Type S :: STRUCTURE_RADIUS
    -- Analytic radius / domain boundary (here: unit circle)

Type I :: INTERACTION_LAW
    -- Integer coefficient law; sequence (a_n) ⊂ ℤ

Type D :: DYNAMICS_OPERATOR
    -- Analytic continuation / meromorphic extension operator

Type f :: ANALYTIC_FUNCTION
    -- Power series with integer coefficients

Type γ :: ARC
    -- Nonempty open subarc of the unit circle B

------------------------------------------------------------
DATA
------------------------------------------------------------
Given:
    f(z) = Σ_{n≥0} a_n z^n
        with a_n ∈ ℤ
        and radius of convergence = 1

Let:
    S := 1
    B := { z : |z| = S }   -- unit circle
    I := (a_n)_{n≥0}       -- integer interaction law
    D := analytic continuation / meromorphic extension of f

------------------------------------------------------------
CORE ANALYTIC FACTS (NON-CIRCULAR)
------------------------------------------------------------
FACT_1 :: ARC_CONTINUATION_TRIGGER
    If there exists a nonempty open arc γ ⊂ B
    such that f admits analytic continuation across γ,
    then f extends meromorphically beyond the disk
    in a neighborhood of γ.

FACT_2 :: POLYA–CARLSON (ANALYTIC + ARITHMETIC)
    Under the integer-coefficient, radius-1 hypothesis:
        If FACT_1 holds (some γ exists),
            then f is rational.

FACT_3 :: FATOU_ARITHMETIC_STRUCTURE
    If f is rational and has integer coefficients,
        then:
            - All poles of f on B lie at roots of unity.
            - The coefficient sequence I = (a_n) satisfies
              a linear recurrence with constant coefficients.
            - After clearing cyclotomic factors, the
              characteristic polynomial has roots on B
              only at roots of unity.

FACT_4 :: NATURAL_BOUNDARY_ALTERNATIVE
    If no such arc γ ⊂ B exists across which f continues,
        then B is a natural boundary:
            - Every point of B is a singularity for f.
            - No analytic continuation of f exists across
              any point of B.

------------------------------------------------------------
RDG DICHOTOMY (STRUCTURED)
------------------------------------------------------------
CASE (A) :: ARC-EXTENDABLE

    Condition:
        ∃ γ ⊂ B, γ open, such that f continues analytically
        across γ.

    Consequences:
        - By FACT_2: f is rational.
        - By FACT_3:
            * Poles of f on B are at roots of unity.
            * I satisfies a linear recurrence with
              constant coefficients.
            * D extends f meromorphically to ℂ minus
              finitely many poles on B.

    RDG view:
        - S = 1 is permeable along γ.
        - I collapses to a finite linear generator
          (recurrence) compatible with roots-of-unity poles.
        - D is globally coherent as a meromorphic operator.

CASE (B) :: NON-ARC-EXTENDABLE

    Condition:
        No nonempty open arc γ ⊂ B exists across which
        f continues analytically.

    Consequences:
        - By FACT_4: B is a natural boundary.
        - Every point of B is singular; no local crossing.

    RDG view:
        - S = 1 is globally sealed.
        - I does not admit a finite meromorphic generator
          compatible with any arc crossing.
        - D is blocked at every boundary point of B.

